1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,516
Hello, and welcome.

2
00:00:01,516 --> 00:00:08,509
This video walks through Stack Scout, an AI powered tech stack intelligence system for business to business sales.

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:24,764
It covers the problem we were given, how the system works from end to end, how artificial intelligence is used and kept honest, what the finished product looks like, how it was built step by step, the problems we hit and how we fixed them, and where its limits are.

4
00:00:24,764 --> 00:00:28,690
It is deployed live on Vercel, and the code is public on GitHub.

5
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:31,155
Here is the problem.

6
00:00:31,155 --> 00:00:36,987
Nikhil is an enterprise account executive at a startup that sells a developer productivity tool.

7
00:00:36,987 --> 00:00:44,703
His product is only relevant in specific contexts: certain cloud platforms, microservices, or particular programming languages.

8
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:52,844
Before a first call he researches each company by hand, reading engineering blogs, job descriptions, conference talks and public code.

9
00:00:52,844 --> 00:01:04,144
For a sprint of twenty companies that takes two to three hours, and the brief says he closes about thirty percent more deals when he mentions something real about how the company builds.

10
00:01:04,144 --> 00:01:11,495
So the task is to keep his watchlist automatically current, and to give him a personalised opening line for each company.

11
00:01:12,745 --> 00:01:15,536
The brief translates into a clear checklist.

12
00:01:15,536 --> 00:01:16,996
Use live external data.

13
00:01:16,996 --> 00:01:19,216
Use Gemini Flash for every AI task.

14
00:01:19,216 --> 00:01:23,785
Show languages, cloud tooling and engineering activity for each company.

15
00:01:23,785 --> 00:01:29,368
Write a two paragraph summary and a first line that is not a fill in the blank template.

16
00:01:29,368 --> 00:01:32,921
Show relevance across cloud, microservices and language.

17
00:01:32,921 --> 00:01:33,873
Refresh weekly.

18
00:01:33,873 --> 00:01:37,680
Let the user add and remove companies without touching code.

19
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,438
And deliver a public Vercel link, a GitHub repository and a components map.

20
00:01:42,438 --> 00:01:46,309
Every one of these is implemented, and the table shows where.

21
00:01:47,559 --> 00:01:49,347
This is the components map.

22
00:01:49,347 --> 00:01:52,063
On the left are four live public sources.

23
00:01:52,063 --> 00:01:54,977
A signal collector gathers them in parallel.

24
00:01:54,977 --> 00:02:00,277
The AI layer, using Gemini Flash, turns the evidence into a structured analysis.

25
00:02:00,277 --> 00:02:06,900
An evidence guard and scoring step then check that analysis, so nothing is claimed without a source.

26
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:11,670
The result is stored in Neon Postgres and displayed by the Next J S app.

27
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:20,016
Vercel Cron triggers the whole pipeline every week, and the same pipeline runs on demand whenever a user refreshes an account.

28
00:02:20,016 --> 00:02:23,791
The bottom lane shows how the buttons in the app call it.

29
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:29,774
The system uses four live public sources, and none of them need a paid key.

30
00:02:29,774 --> 00:02:39,682
From GitHub it finds the company's organisation and reads the language mix, how many repositories were pushed to in the last ninety days, and the star count.

31
00:02:39,682 --> 00:02:45,550
From the company website it reads response headers and script hosts to spot technology hints.

32
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:59,623
From the public job boards of Greenhouse, Lever and Ashby it reads open engineering roles, and goes further by reading up to fifteen job descriptions to count mentions of tools such as AWS, Kubernetes, Go and microservices.

33
00:02:59,623 --> 00:03:05,176
And it discovers the engineering blog's feed to see recent posts and publishing cadence.

34
00:03:05,176 --> 00:03:12,812
One principle runs through all of this: not finding something is never treated as proof that the company does not use it.

35
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:15,985
Now the artificial intelligence.

36
00:03:15,985 --> 00:03:23,018
Every model call in the application goes through a single module, which guarantees only Gemini Flash models are used.

37
00:03:23,018 --> 00:03:26,264
There is no Claude or other model anywhere in the app.

38
00:03:26,264 --> 00:03:30,591
The model is asked for structured J S O N, so the output is predictable.

39
00:03:30,591 --> 00:03:44,176
It returns a technology inventory, a judgement on whether the company runs microservices, five reasons the product fits, three discovery questions for the sales call, the two paragraph summary, and the personalised first line.

40
00:03:44,176 --> 00:03:52,410
That first line can be regenerated on demand, and it always comes with the specific signal it was built on and the source of that signal.

41
00:03:52,410 --> 00:04:01,607
Calls are routed through OpenRouter, locked to Google's models and Google's own infrastructure, with retries and a second Gemini Flash model as fallback.

42
00:04:02,857 --> 00:04:08,692
This is the part we care most about, because language models can sound confident and be wrong.

43
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:11,982
So the model only suggests, and the evidence decides.

44
00:04:11,982 --> 00:04:16,266
Every technology is marked as confirmed or inferred, with its source.

45
00:04:16,266 --> 00:04:25,329
An evidence guard checks the output, and if the model cites a source that was never collected, the claim is downgraded to inferred with no source.

46
00:04:25,329 --> 00:04:32,096
The relevance checklist for cloud, microservices and language shows match, partial, no match, or no evidence.

47
00:04:32,096 --> 00:04:46,312
The fit score is calculated in code from the sourced reasons only: each reason is rated one to three, the sum is divided by fifteen, and a reason without a public source scores zero, so missing evidence can never inflate a score.

48
00:04:47,562 --> 00:04:50,467
For persistence we use Neon serverless Postgres.

49
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:58,334
The target companies table holds the watchlist, the cached analysis, the signals, the fit score, the summary and the opening line.

50
00:04:58,334 --> 00:05:02,026
A research runs table records every refresh and what changed.

51
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:11,769
Notes are stored per company, and a settings table holds the ideal customer profile targets, the stale threshold and the weekly limit, all editable from the app.

52
00:05:11,769 --> 00:05:16,792
One thing worth being upfront about: the brief mentions Supabase, but we used Neon.

53
00:05:16,792 --> 00:05:28,594
Both are Postgres, Neon was set up directly through the Vercel integration, and all database access is in one file, so moving to Supabase would mean swapping the driver and the connection string.

54
00:05:29,844 --> 00:05:32,405
Automation works like this.

55
00:05:32,405 --> 00:05:38,951
Vercel Cron calls a protected endpoint every Monday at six A M U T C.

56
00:05:38,951 --> 00:05:44,738
The endpoint needs a secret, so the public cannot trigger it.

57
00:05:44,738 --> 00:06:01,056
It picks accounts that are new, stale or failed, oldest first, up to a saved weekly limit, and researches two at a time inside a time budget so the function never overruns.

58
00:06:01,056 --> 00:06:04,756
Each run is recorded with what changed.

59
00:06:04,756 --> 00:06:12,251
Stale means older than seven days by default, and that is editable in settings.

60
00:06:12,251 --> 00:06:16,520
Users can also refresh any account on demand.

61
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:22,118
In our tests one account costs about two cents of AI usage.

62
00:06:23,368 --> 00:06:24,727
Now the product itself.

63
00:06:24,727 --> 00:06:26,679
The Overview is a command centre.

64
00:06:26,679 --> 00:06:35,902
The hero metric is the number of strong ideal customer profile matches, with a breakdown of how many accounts match three, two, one or none of the criteria.

65
00:06:35,902 --> 00:06:39,036
The main area is a queue of recommended next actions.

66
00:06:39,036 --> 00:06:40,750
It is not just a leaderboard.

67
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:49,264
It mixes accounts that are ready for outreach, accounts where fit evidence still needs completing, and accounts whose research needs refreshing.

68
00:06:49,264 --> 00:06:56,950
Each item shows the match indicators, a one line reason taken from the evidence, how fresh the research is, and a clear next step.

69
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:06,332
Further down, a feed shows what changed: score movements detected on refresh and new engineering blog posts, all computed from stored records.

70
00:07:06,332 --> 00:07:08,336
Three charts answer real questions.

71
00:07:08,336 --> 00:07:12,803
Which clouds and languages does my watchlist run on, compared with my targets?

72
00:07:12,803 --> 00:07:15,322
And which high fit accounts are also hiring?

73
00:07:15,322 --> 00:07:22,939
Accounts with no public job board are marked unknown rather than plotted as zero, because we never present missing data as a finding.

74
00:07:24,189 --> 00:07:26,759
The Accounts page is the working watchlist.

75
00:07:26,759 --> 00:07:36,023
You can search by name or domain, switch between five saved views, filter by match, fit, status, freshness, cloud and language, and sort by several fields.

76
00:07:36,023 --> 00:07:42,896
Users can add a company, remove one with a confirmation step, refresh research, or run bulk actions on a selection.

77
00:07:42,896 --> 00:07:48,276
All of the filter state lives in the web address, so any view can be shared or bookmarked.

78
00:07:48,276 --> 00:07:58,137
Clicking a row opens a quick view drawer so you keep your place in the list, and the full profile remembers which list you came from, with previous and next buttons.

79
00:07:59,387 --> 00:08:00,936
Each company gets a dossier.

80
00:08:00,936 --> 00:08:09,065
At the top is the two paragraph engineering context written by Gemini, with the public sources it used linked underneath so anyone can verify them.

81
00:08:09,065 --> 00:08:10,890
On the right is the opening line.

82
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:18,190
Beneath it you can see exactly which signal the line is built on and where that signal came from, and you can regenerate or copy it.

83
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:25,103
There are also three discovery questions the public evidence cannot answer, and key numbers that are computed, not generated.

84
00:08:26,353 --> 00:08:33,976
Lower on the same page, the fit explanation shows each of the three criteria with its evidence and a link to the source.

85
00:08:33,976 --> 00:08:41,853
Every fit reason can be expanded to see the evidence behind it, and missing evidence is shown explicitly rather than hidden.

86
00:08:41,853 --> 00:08:52,272
The technology map groups what was found into cloud, languages, and frameworks, with real logos, and clicking any technology reveals the evidence and source for it.

87
00:08:52,272 --> 00:09:01,039
There is a language share bar, engineering activity, and a methodology panel explaining how the research was done and how confident it is.

88
00:09:02,289 --> 00:09:05,170
Three supporting workspaces complete the product.

89
00:09:05,170 --> 00:09:15,754
Research and Signals shows coverage across the whole watchlist in a matrix with five honest states: evidence found, partial, no evidence found, not researched, and research failed.

90
00:09:15,754 --> 00:09:19,635
It also shows freshness, evidence gaps and a log of every refresh.

91
00:09:19,635 --> 00:09:28,220
Outreach Studio is a focused page where you choose a company, review the observations a line could rest on, and generate and copy an opening line.

92
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:37,628
Settings lets the user edit the ideal customer profile targets, the stale threshold and the weekly limit, and these really are saved and used by the weekly job.

93
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:39,465
On design.

94
00:09:39,465 --> 00:09:50,960
The interface evolved through three versions: a simple dashboard first, then a light editorial design from a design handoff, then the premium dark system you see now, which also has a light theme.

95
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:53,717
Design patterns were used only where they help.

96
00:09:53,717 --> 00:10:04,978
A bento layout for the overview, command centre style actions, Swiss grid style tables, a master and detail drawer for company research, and progressive disclosure so detail is one click away.

97
00:10:04,978 --> 00:10:11,429
Glass blur is used only for floating elements like the command palette and dialogs, never on tables or charts.

98
00:10:11,429 --> 00:10:20,403
Every chart answers a real question, there are no invented trends, and the app works from a phone to a desktop with accessible labels and keyboard focus.

99
00:10:21,653 --> 00:10:23,160
Here is how it was built.

100
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:31,901
We started by reading the brief and choosing the stack: Next J S with Tailwind, Neon for the database, Gemini for the AI, and Vercel for hosting.

101
00:10:31,901 --> 00:10:37,628
Then came the database, the GitHub signal collector, the first Gemini analysis and a dashboard.

102
00:10:37,628 --> 00:10:41,667
Next the repository, the Vercel deployment and the Neon connection.

103
00:10:41,667 --> 00:10:48,659
We added job boards and themes, then the weekly cron, the relevance checklist, and blog and job description signals.

104
00:10:48,659 --> 00:10:53,723
The interface was redesigned twice, and the AI calls were routed through OpenRouter.

105
00:10:53,723 --> 00:11:03,007
Later came charts, the command palette, the quick view drawer, and research on twenty real companies, followed by the components map and this walkthrough.

106
00:11:03,007 --> 00:11:10,060
It was built iteratively with an A I coding assistant, and every step was type checked, built, deployed and verified.

107
00:11:11,310 --> 00:11:15,860
Not everything worked first time, and these are the real problems we hit.

108
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:23,027
Deployments were blocked because the commit email was not linked to GitHub, so we used the GitHub no reply address.

109
00:11:23,027 --> 00:11:28,200
The Gemini model we first chose was retired, so we updated it and added a fallback.

110
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:33,935
Overload and quota errors led to retries, a fallback model and plain English error messages.

111
00:11:33,935 --> 00:11:40,292
When an A P I key was rejected, we routed the calls through OpenRouter, restricted to Google's models.

112
00:11:40,292 --> 00:11:46,088
Older records crashed pages after the data model grew, so defaults are now filled in on read.

113
00:11:46,088 --> 00:11:54,066
A client bundle accidentally included the database driver, a mobile layout overflowed, and a font feature distorted punctuation.

114
00:11:54,066 --> 00:11:55,500
We fixed each of these.

115
00:11:55,500 --> 00:12:00,486
And Vercel occasionally did not start a deployment, which a fresh push resolved.

116
00:12:01,736 --> 00:12:03,130
How do we know it works?

117
00:12:03,130 --> 00:12:06,672
Every change was type checked and built before it was pushed.

118
00:12:06,672 --> 00:12:12,596
Twenty real companies were researched live on the deployed app, taking fifteen to twenty seconds each.

119
00:12:12,596 --> 00:12:24,618
We tested interactions in a real browser: that filter counts match the overview, that the quick view steps through the list, that the command palette and sidebar behave, and that settings save and read back.

120
00:12:24,618 --> 00:12:29,554
Layouts were checked at phone, tablet and desktop widths with no horizontal overflow.

121
00:12:29,554 --> 00:12:37,685
In production every route returns success, and the A I paths for research, regenerating a line and saving settings were verified end to end.

122
00:12:38,935 --> 00:12:41,791
It is important to be honest about the limits.

123
00:12:41,791 --> 00:12:47,750
The system only sees public signals, so private repositories and internal tooling are invisible.

124
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:52,530
The phrase no evidence found does not mean absent, and the interface says so.

125
00:12:52,530 --> 00:12:59,235
Job boards are discovered by guessing the board name from the domain, so some companies show no hiring data.

126
00:12:59,235 --> 00:13:03,146
GitHub data covers the sixty most recently active repositories.

127
00:13:03,146 --> 00:13:09,602
Model judgements can be wrong, which is exactly why every claim carries a source and a confidence level.

128
00:13:09,602 --> 00:13:17,920
There are no trend charts yet because run history only started recently, and the app has no login because it is public for evaluation.

129
00:13:19,170 --> 00:13:23,106
Finally, some questions you may be asked, with short answers.

130
00:13:23,106 --> 00:13:24,202
Why Gemini Flash?

131
00:13:24,202 --> 00:13:29,234
It is fast, costs about two cents per account, and supports structured output.

132
00:13:29,234 --> 00:13:31,169
How do you stop hallucination?

133
00:13:31,169 --> 00:13:38,459
Claims need a collected source, an evidence guard downgrades the rest, and the score counts only sourced reasons.

134
00:13:38,459 --> 00:13:40,136
Why Neon and not Supabase?

135
00:13:40,136 --> 00:13:47,813
Both are Postgres, Neon was already connected through Vercel, and swapping needs only the driver and connection string.

136
00:13:47,813 --> 00:13:49,942
How does the weekly refresh work?

137
00:13:49,942 --> 00:13:56,458
Vercel Cron calls a secret protected endpoint on Mondays and refreshes new, stale or failed accounts.

138
00:13:56,458 --> 00:13:58,393
And what would you build next?

139
00:13:58,393 --> 00:14:05,167
Trend charts from run history, a login, C R M sync, conference talk signals and wider job board coverage.

140
00:14:06,417 --> 00:14:10,432
That is Stack Scout, from the problem statement to a deployed product.

141
00:14:10,432 --> 00:14:15,250
The live app, the public repository and the components map are all linked on screen.

142
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:18,749
Thank you for watching, and I am happy to take any questions.
